  "name": "LAP SCORE LEUCOCYTE ALKALINE PHOSPHATASE",
  "code": "1136",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"68\" data-end=\"106\"><strong data-start=\"68\" data-end=\"106\">Purpose and Clinical Significance:</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"107\" data-end=\"515\">\n<li data-start=\"107\" data-end=\"195\">\n<p data-start=\"109\" data-end=\"195\">The LAP score measures the activity of <strong data-start=\"148\" data-end=\"194\">alkaline phosphatase enzyme in neutrophils</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"196\" data-end=\"418\">\n<p data-start=\"198\" data-end=\"418\">Primarily used to <strong data-start=\"216\" data-end=\"276\">differentiate causes of elevated white blood cell counts</strong>, especially to distinguish between <strong data-start=\"312\" data-end=\"346\">chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)</strong> and <strong data-start=\"351\" data-end=\"376\">reactive neutrophilia</strong> (e.g., due to infection or inflammation).</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"419\" data-end=\"515\">\n<p data-start=\"421\" data-end=\"515\">Helpful in evaluating <strong data-start=\"443\" data-end=\"475\">myeloproliferative disorders</strong> and certain <strong data-start=\"488\" data-end=\"514\">hematologic conditions</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>LAP Score</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Elevated (&gt;130)</strong></td>\n<td>Suggestive of <strong>leukemoid reaction</strong>, <strong>polycythemia vera</strong>, <strong>pregnancy</strong>, or <strong>infections</strong>. Indicates a reactive process rather than leukemia.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low (&lt;20)</strong></td>\n<td>Seen in <strong>chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)</strong> and sometimes in <strong>paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)</strong>. Reflects decreased neutrophil enzyme activity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normal (20&ndash;130)</strong></td>\n<td>Generally considered <strong>non-specific</strong>, but can help rule out extremes. Further evaluation is needed if clinically indicated.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
